        "en": {
          "research_question": "The authors sought to catalog worldwide research on antibody tracking for zoonotic influenza across animal reservoirs and at-risk human cohorts over a seven-year period.",
          "study_design": "A systematic scoping review was conducted using PRISMA-ScR protocols to evaluate peer-reviewed literature indexed in PubMed.",
          "population_setting": "Eligible records covered thirty-three nations and documented twenty-five distinct animal taxa, with particular emphasis on agricultural livestock and workers with direct animal contact.",
          "main_findings": "Analysis of ninety qualifying papers demonstrated that current antibody monitoring predominantly targets poultry and pigs, leaving wildlife and companion species significantly understudied. While advanced testing formats such as multiplex and nanobody assays emerged, practical adoption continues to encounter operational hurdles.",
          "public_health_relevance": "Expanding antibody-based monitoring programs across interconnected health sectors may improve early identification of cross-species viral circulation and strengthen global outbreak readiness.",
          "limitations": "The authors note that restricting searches to one repository, excluding non-English works, omitting formal study quality evaluation, and encountering high methodological diversity constrain the review's comprehensiveness.",
          "gids_interpretation": "This work functions as a bibliometric and methodological inventory of historical antibody tracking initiatives for avian and swine influenza, offering context on regional coverage and technical evolution without referencing active outbreak monitoring.",
